{"name":"Dry Starfish","hex":"#b09a77","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Darai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free from or lacking moisture.","Unable to produce a liquid, as water,  oil, or  milk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dry"},{"term":"starfish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various echinoderms (not in fact fish) with usually five arms, many of which eat bivalves or corals by everting their stomach.","Any many-armed or tentacled sea invertebrate, whether cnidarian, echinoderm, or cephalopod."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To assume a splayed-out shape, like that of a starfish.\n\nWhile floating on water","While floating on water"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/starfish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Starfish","blocks":[{"text":"Starfish or sea stars are a class of echinoderms generally shaped like a star polygon. Starfish are also known as asteroids because they form the taxonomic class Asteroidea. About 1,900 species of starfish live on the seabed, and are found in all the world's oceans, from warm, tropical zones to frigid, polar regions. They can occur from the intertidal zone down to abyssal depths, at 6,000 m (20,000 ft) below the surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Starfish_montage.png/330px-Starfish_montage.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starfish","wikidata":"Q25349"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg/330px-Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg/960px-Luidia_maculata%2C_Ras_Sedr%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip